  "raw": " usual amount, and one out of four of those fathers took no leave at all.\n\nThis is a particularly striking finding since, in 2013, the UK became the first developed country to enshrine a statutory five-week entitlement to paid paternity leave to all new fathers. The UK Government has pledged, too, to provide fathers with two weeks of paid paternity leave – though the final entitlement is due by the end of the current parliamentary cycle in 2020.\n\nFor many, though, access to time off work is the easy part. Some of those who do not avail themselves of their rights to paternity leave admit that they have no idea how to negotiate paternity leave entitlements for themselves or their partners. They worry about cost, timing and stress over how they would share care.\n\nOur study, published in the Journal of Social Issues, highlights the importance of a positive workplace culture in promoting men in the work environment, and in helping them understand how paternity leave work for other people.\n",
  "serialized": "usual amount, and one out of four of those fathers took no leave at all. This is a particularly striking finding since, in 2013, the UK became the first developed country to enshrine a statutory five-week entitlement to paid paternity leave to all new fathers. The UK Government has pledged, too, to provide fathers with two weeks of paid paternity leave – though the final entitlement is due by the end of the current parliamentary cycle in 2020. For many, though, access to time off work is the easy part. Some of those who do not avail themselves of their rights to paternity leave admit that they have no idea how to negotiate paternity leave entitlements for themselves or their partners. They worry about cost, timing and stress over how they would share care. Our study, published in the Journal of Social Issues, highlights the importance of a positive workplace culture in promoting men in the work environment, and in helping them understand how paternity leave work for other people.",
